<header><p class="enc-kicker">Religion / idea · deity</p><h1 class="enc-title">Rama</h1></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Rama is a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hinduism">Hindu</a> god, the seventh avatar of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vishnu">Vishnu</a> and the hero of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sanskrit">Sanskrit</a> epic <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ramayana">Ramayana</a>. In the epic he is a prince of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ayodhya">Ayodhya</a> in Kosala, of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ikshvaku">Ikshvaku</a> line, who is exiled to the forest, rescues his wife <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sita">Sita</a> from Ravana, the demon king of Lanka, and returns to rule in an ideal reign. His story is retold across South and South-<a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/east-asia">East Asia</a>, and his worship is central to Vaishnava devotion.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/rama.jpg" alt="Rama" width="320" height="522" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Gita Press Gorakhpur · CC0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Rama_in_Adhyatma_Ramayana.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>The name joins the Gurjaras, a people and later a social group widely present in the region, whose origins are debated, with the title pratihara, ‘doorkeeper’; the dynasty’s own Gwalior inscription traced its descent from Lakshmana, who served as doorkeeper to his brother <b>Rama</b>.</blockquote><a href="/empire/gurjara-pratihara">Gurjara-Pratihara · Foundation</a></li><li><blockquote>Between them stood officials titled Rakai or Samget, landed gentry who ruled a watak of several villages and passed the king's orders to the <b>Rama</b>, the village heads.</blockquote><a href="/empire/medang-kingdom">Medang Kingdom · Governance &amp; Law</a></li><li><blockquote>The major traditions are devoted to Vishnu, who descends to earth in incarnations (avatars) such as <b>Rama</b> and Krishna; to Shiva, the ascetic and the lord of creation and destruction; and to the Goddess, Devi, in forms such as Durga, Kali, Lakshmi and Parvati.</blockquote><a href="/religion/hinduism">Hinduism · Beliefs &amp; Practice</a></li><li><blockquote>…longest poems in the world, tells of the war between two branches of a royal family and contains the Bhagavad Gita, the dialogue between the warrior Arjuna and the god Krishna; the Ramayana, attributed to the sage Valmiki, tells the story of Prince <b>Rama</b> and his wife Sita.</blockquote><a href="/language/sanskrit">Sanskrit · Literature &amp; Learning</a></li><li><blockquote>Tulsidas recorded in the Ramcharitmanas itself that he began the poem at Ayodhya in 1574, on the festival of <b>Rama</b>’s birth; the Ramlila plays still staged across northern India each autumn are based on its verses.</blockquote><a href="/movement/bhakti-movement">Bhakti movement · At a glance</a></li><li><blockquote>Tulsidas, devoted to <b>Rama</b>, is best known for the Ramcharitmanas, his retelling of the Ramayana in the Awadhi vernacular.</blockquote><a href="/songs/hanuman-chalisa">Hanuman Chalisa</a></li></ul></section>
